The Critical Finding
WARNING

Your @Version annotation on WalletEntity ALREADY protects against the race condition that the Redis lock is trying to prevent.

If two threads read wallet balance = ₹1000 and both try to save:

Thread A saves with version=1 → succeeds, version becomes 2
Thread B tries to save with version=1 → throws OptimisticLockException → transaction rolls back
The data is NEVER corrupted. The second debit simply fails.

What Redis Lock Adds ON TOP of @Version
Without Redis Lock (only @Version)	With Redis Lock
Both threads read from DB, do work	Second thread fails immediately ("Wallet is busy")
Second thread fails at save time (wasted work)	Second thread doesn't even start work
Exception type: OptimisticLockException	Exception type: RuntimeException("Wallet is busy")
User sees generic error	User sees clear "busy" message
Failed thread already created LedgerEntry that must be rolled back	No wasted DB writes
Verdict: Redis Lock is a performance optimization and better UX layer on top of @Version. It fails-fast instead of doing wasted work. But @Version is the actual safety net that prevents data corruption.

